
-------------------------------------------------------------
                      Shallow && Deep Copy
--------------------------------------------------------------
🔍 Shallow Copy (Important Points)
Creates new object
But internal objects are NOT copied
References are shared
👉 Meaning:
Change in one → affects other
-----------------------------------------------
🔍 Deep Copy (Important Points)
Creates new object
Also creates new copy of internal objects
No shared references
👉 Meaning:
Change in one → does NOT affect other

example:
🔹 Step 1: Class
class Address {
    String city;

    Address(String city) {
        this.city = city;
    }
}

class Student {
    int id;
    Address address;

    Student(int id, Address address) {
        this.id = id;
        this.address = address;
    }
}

🔥 1. Shallow Copy Example
public class ShallowCopyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Address addr = new Address("Pune");
        Student s1 = new Student(1, addr);

        // Shallow copy
        Student s2 = s1;

        // change in s2
        s2.address.city = "Mumbai";

        System.out.println(s1.address.city); // Mumbai ❗
        System.out.println(s2.address.city); // Mumbai
    }
}

👉 Output:
Mumbai
Mumbai


👉 Why?

Both using same Address object
🔥 2. Deep Copy Example
public class DeepCopyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Address addr1 = new Address("Pune");
        Student s1 = new Student(1, addr1);

        // Deep copy (manual)
        Address addr2 = new Address(s1.address.city);
        Student s2 = new Student(s1.id, addr2);

        // change in s2
        s2.address.city = "Mumbai";

        System.out.println(s1.address.city); // Pune ✅
        System.out.println(s2.address.city); // Mumbai
    }
}

👉 Output:
Pune
Mumbai


👉 Why?

Different Address objects